  "body": "Reported by a user on Discord (f1inch), relayed by the maintainer's collaborator on 2026-11-16; filed by an agent:\n\n> \"after i add a second operation after faceing the camcraft gets so damn slow that i cant work with it.\"\n\nThe user shared the project privately. It is not attached here.\n\n## The project (shape only)\n\n- Project version 61, 17 MB on disk.\n- One model of about 45,000 triangles (8.4 MB of facets).\n- One setup, one box stock, one tool (4 mm router bit).\n- Two operations: `Face1`, then `2D Contour1`.\n- Machine: a hobby router file (FoxAlien WM-3020), grblHAL post.\n\n## Readings (headless, release build of main 3580f3888, Linux)\n\n| Step | Time |\n|---|---|\n| `open_project` | 0.1 s |\n| `generate` 2D Contour1 (forced) | 1.0 s |\n| `generate` Face1 (forced) | < 0.1 s |\n| `verify` | 0.2 s |\n\nSo project load, generation and verification are fast. The slowness is in the windowed shell: the redraw, the scene rebuild, the toolpath or stock display, or the simulation. #164 (closed) found a similar pattern: one CPU core at 100% in the window while `camcraft-cli process` took under a second.\n\nThe project also has two problems that are not this defect:\n- The model was saved before mesh fidelity was recorded (v58 or older), so the gouge check blocks the post until the model is imported again.\n- `Face1` moves go outside the machine stroke (X to -47.5, Z to -4, stroke X 0..290, Z 0..70): 626 limit problems.\n\nA large number of findings can make a UI list slow. Check if the window rebuilds or paints all 626 findings each frame.\n\n## What is not known yet\n\n- Which action is slow: select an operation, regenerate, rotate the view, or play the simulation.\n- The user's CamCraft version, operating system and GPU (the paths suggest Linux).\n- If one operation is fast and two operations are slow.\n\n## Done means\n\nOn a fixture with the same shape (a model of about 45,000 triangles, a Face and a 2D Contour, and several hundred findings), the windowed UI stays responsive: a frame cost close to the one-operation case, no CPU core held at 100% when idle. A test pins the cost that caused it (for example the scene or findings rebuild count for each frame).\n",
